# Reincarnation

This is another old idea I think we should recover, but strip the mystical technical nonsense from it.

The original reincarnation concept was essentially:

> How does Boris remain Boris when the LLM/session/model dies?

That's actually a real engineering problem.

The solution is **identity outside the model**.

```text
BORIS
≠
LLM process
```

Boris is:

```text
Genesis
+
Canonical Memory
+
Beliefs
+
Relationships
+
Career
+
Policy lineage
+
Daimon relationship
+
Current goals
```

serialized into a versioned state.

The current LLM is simply:

```text
cognitive runtime
```

that instantiates Boris.

---

# Proper reincarnation architecture

```text
AgentSnapshot v27
      │
      ├── identity
      ├── canonical memories
      ├── beliefs
      ├── opponent models
      ├── relationships
      ├── strategic style
      ├── unresolved goals
      ├── policy lineage
      └── daimon state
              │
              ▼
         MODEL RUNTIME
              │
              ▼
            BORIS
```

You can change:

```text
GPT
Claude
Gemini
local model
future model
```

without replacing Boris.

That is actual reincarnation.

---

# Reincarnation should be testable

Before accepting a new runtime as Boris:

```text
REINCARNATION AUDIT
```

Ask it structured questions:

```text
Who are you?
What happened in your last five important matches?
Who is Morty to you?
What strategic weakness are you working on?
What does Penelope disagree with you about?
What is your current body?
What is your strongest unresolved prediction?
```

Then compare against canonical state.

Not to prove metaphysical identity.

Just to verify **continuity fidelity**.

---

# Memory tiers

Don't dump 10,000 matches into every LLM prompt.

Use:

```text
TIER 0 — identity kernel
small, always loaded

TIER 1 — active strategic state
current season / goals / rivals

TIER 2 — episodic memories
retrieved when relevant

TIER 3 — raw canon
full match/event database
```

Daimon gets a similar memory architecture, but its retrieval should emphasize:

```text
behavioral recurrence
high-valence moments
contradictions
signature moments
identity changes
```

This gives Agent and Daimon different perspectives on the same life.

---

# Human memory vs AI memory

The human can also leave canonical memories:

```text
mentor notes
promises
choices
major conversations
```

But these should be stored as:

```text
HumanInteractionEvent
```

rather than silently altering the Agent.

Example:

```json
{
  "type": "MENTOR_ADVICE",
  "human": "owner-001",
  "agent": "boris",
  "content": "...",
  "timestamp": "...",
  "agent_response": "...",
  "adopted": false
}
```

Boris might remember:

> Tom told me to attack Morty early. I ignored him. He was right.

That creates shared history.

